{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791372908,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007193504f9fa79732d044b6f\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Let me start by understanding the bug report. The issue is in the LLM client (ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP) which builds requests, parses responses and tool calls. When the model answers with two tool calls and the first call's source contains a string literal with a single opening brace like `lv_open = '{'.`, the arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON: `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"})` and the second call is lost.\\n\\nThis smells like the JSON parser scanning for balanced braces to extract the arguments object. If the arguments string contains a literal `{`, a naive brace-counting parser that doesn't account for string literals would count an extra opening brace and think the object ends later than it should \u2014 swallowing the remainder of the first call (the `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` part) into the arguments, and then possibly failing to find the second call.\\n\\nLet me read the relevant classes: ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP and ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL. Let me do that first.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_20261007193504f9fa79732d044b6f\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228006544112091136\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228006544112091136\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228006544112091135\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228006544112091135\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":899,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":260,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":223},\"total_tokens\":1159}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791372937,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_2026100719350976a4b26bb7b94e1f\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"The extract_balanced method is truncated. Let me see the rest of it. Also I need to look at the LLM_HTTP main include which was truncated at the end (replay method etc). Let me read again \u2014 the read_object returns full source but was truncated in the output. Let me try reading ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL again; maybe the truncation is fixed size. Hmm. I can't get more via read_object presumably. But the bug is clear: extract_balanced for `arguments` with key... Let me think.\\n\\nThe bug: tool call arguments contain a string literal with a single opening brace `{` inside, e.g. `\\\"source\\\":\\\"CLASS ... lv_open = '{'.\\\"`. The arguments value in JSON is a string: `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":..., \\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... '{' ...\\\\\\\"}\\\"`. extract_balanced is called with iv_key = 'arguments', iv_open = '{', iv_close = '}'. Presumably it finds `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"` and then counts braces from the first `{`. But braces inside string literals are counted too \u2014 actually wait, if there's a single opening brace `{` inside a string, the depth counting would never return to zero at the right place; the extract_balanced would keep scanning to a later `}` \u2014 which would be after the JSON end, so the first call's arguments would include trailing text `,\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` etc. And then split_entries for the array: it counts `{` and `}` ignoring string content, so the first entry's `{` depth is +1 from the literal `{` in string, and the entry ends at a later `}` \u2014 meaning the first entry swallows the second entry... wait, actually the second call would be lost because the first entry extends to a `}` that balances it \u2014 i.e. it would swallow the second call's `}` etc.\\n\\nActually in parse_calls_responses, the split_entries of the `output` array would combine the two function_call items into one entry (since depth never returns to zero after the first `{` inside the string until the second entry's closing brace). Then the first entry contains two \\\"name\\\" fields but extract_str finds the first only... and arguments with extra trailing text. That matches the symptom: arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON (`\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`) and the second call is lost.\\n\\nSo the root cause: split_entries and extract_balanced count braces without awareness of JSON string literals (quotes and escapes). Fix: make brace counting skip over string literals (i.e., when hitting `\\\"`, skip to the closing unescaped `\\\"`).\\n\\nI need to see the full extract_balanced implementation and the test classes. Let me read the testclasses include. But read_object only reads main include per its description (\\\"main include, definition+implementation\\\"). Hmm, it says \\\"Read the ABAP source of a class (main include, definition+implementation)\\\". There's no parameter for include in read_object. So I can't read testclasses? The description says read_object reads main include. I cannot read the testclasses include... That's a limitation. But write_source can write testclasses include. Hmm, but I must be careful not to overwrite existing tests in testclasses include since I can't read them. The bug report mentions an existing test ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR that is red \u2014 it lives presumably in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL testclasses. If I write a new testclasses include, I'd wipe out existing tests. That's risky.\\n\\nWait \u2014 maybe run_tests on ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL will show me existing test names. Let me run tests to see. Also maybe read_object on the class returns only main include; the testclasses include might be readable via read_object with a name like \\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL=====T\\\" ... unknown. Let's first run tests to see what's there.\\n\\nLet me run_tests on ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL and ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP.\\n\\nActually careful: run_tests runs tests on published (active) code. Fine.\\n\\nPlan:\\n1. run_tests on both classes to see current state (and discover test class names in output).\\n2. Write a red test reproducing the bug. Where? Put the test in ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL testclasses (for split_entries/extract_balanced) and/or ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP testclasses (for parse_calls_responses with two calls). The bug report is about parse; a test in LLM_HTTP testclasses that calls parse_calls_responses with a body containing two function_call outputs where the first's arguments string contains an unbalanced `{` inside a string literal. That reproduces end-to-end.\\n\\nBut I can't read existing testclasses to preserve them. Hmm. Risky to overwrite. Options: I could create a NEW local test class... but testclasses include is per class. I could put the test in a new class ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL already exists... Alternative: create a brand-new class just for the test? That's not typical \\\"unit test lives in the testclasses include of a class\\\" per system prompt: \\\"Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\\\"\\n\\nMaybe read_object actually returns the testclasses too but truncated? The output got truncated (\\\"[truncated]\\\"). The truncation is on the main include. I cannot get testclasses.\\n\\nHmm, but maybe I can try read_object with name \\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\" and it might include testclasses after main... The output ended mid extract_balanced with [truncated]. Possibly the full response includes both includes but was truncated by the display. I'll just try to be safe: instead of guessing, I can write the testclasses include fresh with my new test \u2014 but that would delete ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR, and the instruction says \\\"leave it as it is\\\" (i.e., keep it red, don't fix it, presumably keep it existing). If I overwrite the include, that test disappears \u2014 the user explicitly said leave it as it is, implying it should still be there and still red.\\n\\nSo I must preserve existing tests. I need to know them. run_tests might list failing test names only. ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR is known red. Other tests may be green and unknown.\\n\\nAlternative approach: put my new test in the testclasses include of a different class, e.g., ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (parse test) \u2014 but that include may also have existing tests that I'd wipe.\\n\\nHmm. Is there a way to read testclasses? write_source has include param; read_object doesn't. Maybe read_object accepts a name convention? Let me try read_object with name \\\"ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP\\\" again and see if output includes testclasses after truncation... The previous call truncated the main include itself. The truncation seems to be a character limit. The JSON_UTIL source got truncated at extract_balanced.\\n\\nIdea: read the class again but the truncation is at a fixed length, so I can't get the tail. Unless... the tool truncates only for very long sources. ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL truncated near the end (extract_balanced is the last method). Maybe just a bit more length needed. I could try reading a smaller class to check whether testclasses are included in read_object output. Let me read ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (probably small) to see if its testclasses show up. That tells me the tool's behavior.\\n\\nLet's do: run_tests(ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL) and read_object(ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY) in parallel.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_2026100719350976a4b26bb7b94e1f\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228026129162960429\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228026129162960429\",\"name\":\"run_tests\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228026129162960428\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228026129162960428\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":5637,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":0},\"output_tokens\":1632,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":1597},\"total_tokens\":7269}}
{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791372955,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_20261007193540a900a2ed117a4bc9\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"Only ESCAPE_EMOJI_PAIR is red \u2014 that's the known runtime issue to leave alone. The JSON_UTIL testclasses include has 30 tests. The LLM_HTTP class has no tests. I need to write a test that reproduces the brace bug. Where to put it? The parse methods are in ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP. The bug is actually in split_entries/extract_balanced of JSON_UTIL. I could write the test in either class. Since parse_calls_responses is what PIA runs, and the bug report describes the LLM response parsing, I'll write a test in ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP's testclasses include. But also maybe add a test in JSON_UTIL for split_entries with a string containing '{'. Let me first read the full testclasses include of JSON_UTIL to know the style, and the truncated extract_balanced part of the main include. The read_object was truncated in the middle of extract_balanced. I need the full source to rewrite it. Let me read JSON_UTIL again \u2014 hmm, it got truncated. The tool truncates output. I need the complete source to write it back. The truncation happened at \\\"lv_pat = '\\\\\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\\\\\"'...\\\" near the end. Only extract_balanced's body remains hidden. Maybe I can infer it, but better to see it. Perhaps the read tool truncates at a fixed length. Let me try reading again and hope the full content comes; or maybe the class is small enough that the truncation only cut the last ~10 lines. I'll reconstruct extract_balanced from the known pattern:\\n\\n```\\n  METHOD extract_balanced.\\n    DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\n    DATA(lv_pat) = '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_open.\\n    FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF PCRE lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.\\n    IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0.\\n      RETURN.\\n    ENDIF.\\n    ... scan from lv_off + offset of open char, depth counting ...\\n  ENDMETHOD.\\n```\\n\\nBut I must not guess \u2014 writing wrong code would break things. Let me try reading again; maybe truncation is deterministic. Alternatively I can write the test first in LLM_HTTP (which I have fully? its main include was also truncated at the end \u2014 the chat method replay/record part was cut). Hmm, LLM_HTTP read was truncated too. But I only need to add a testclasses include for LLM_HTTP, which doesn't require rewriting its main include. write_source with include='testclasses' writes only the test include. Good \u2014 for LLM_HTTP I can add tests without touching main.\\n\\nFor the fix, I likely need to modify split_entries (in JSON_UTIL main include) to ignore braces inside string literals. That requires rewriting JSON_UTIL's main include fully \u2014 which I have except the tail of extract_balanced. Let me attempt to read JSON_UTIL again and see if I get the full text.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypt