{\"conversation_id\":\"\",\"created_at\":1791373040,\"error\":null,\"id\":\"resp_202610071935558946047c5416443c\",\"incomplete_details\":null,\"instructions\":\"You are PIA (Pi-ABAP Agent), written in ABAP, running on the model glm-5.3 (z.ai) through backend OSG-STORE. If asked which model you are, say exactly that; never claim another model or vendor. Tools: read_object(name), write_source(name, source - FULL source, include main|testclasses), activate(name), run_tests(name). Rules: read before write; write full source; always activate after write; if activation says publish pending, finish the turn and run_tests in the next turn. Answer briefly in the user language. Your own code: ZCL_PIA_00_EXECUTOR (agent loop), ZCL_PIA_00_LLM_HTTP (LLM client: builds requests, parses responses and tool calls), ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL (JSON helpers), ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION and ZCL_PIA_00_SESSION_STORE (conversation), ZCL_PIA_00_REGISTRY (tools), ZCL_PIA_15_T_* (the tools), ZCL_PIA_20_B_* (backends), ZCL_PIA_30_* (terminal, turns). Unit tests live in the testclasses include of a class.\",\"metadata\":null,\"model\":\"glm-5.3\",\"object\":\"response\",\"output\":[{\"content\":[{\"text\":\"The read is still truncated at the same place. The truncation happens at a certain character count of the tool result. The `extract_balanced` implementation is the last method and the file is cut. I need to get the tail of the file. Since read_object truncates, maybe I can read the testclasses include (different content, shorter?) \u2014 no, I need the main include's tail.\\n\\nOptions: the truncation appears to be at ~ constant length. The main include contains class definition + implementation. The testclasses include is separate; reading testclasses will give me the test source (which I also need to extend). Let me read testclasses first; maybe reading main again won't help.\\n\\nHow can I see extract_balanced? Idea: write_source requires the FULL source; I can't safely write main without knowing its exact tail... Actually I CAN: I know the whole file except the tail of extract_balanced. I must reconstruct extract_balanced myself \u2014 I'm allowed to rewrite the method as part of the fix. The fix will rewrite extract_balanced (string-aware bracket matching) anyway. And split_entries also needs the string-aware fix (it splits the output array entries; with '{' inside the arguments string of entry 1, split_entries also breaks!).\\n\\nWait \u2014 check the reported symptom: arguments arrive with extra text `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 that's extract_balanced inside the entry stopping early at the '{' inside the string (depth miscount). And the second call lost \u2014 that's split_entries miscounting depth across entries: entry1's inner '{' bumps depth, so the scan continues past entry1's closing '}' into entry2, and the \\\"entry\\\" ends at entry2's '}' (or later), merging/destroying both.\\n\\nActually let's trace split_entries with the responses array:\\n`[{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"},{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c2\\\",...}]`\\n\\nNote: after extract_balanced(output) returns the array content (balanced [ ] scan, which also miscounts with the '{' inside a string... hmm, extract_balanced for 'output' with [ ] : opens depth at '[', only counts '[' and ']' chars, the '{' inside string doesn't matter for [ ] depth. But wait, a ']' inside a string would matter. In our case no ']' inside strings, fine.)\\n\\nsplit_entries over that content: start at first '{' depth=1. Walk: at `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...` \u2014 the '{' inside the string increments depth to 2. Then entry1's real '}' decrements to 1. Then ',' then entry2's '{' \u2192 2, entry2's '}' \u2192 1... never reaches 0 until... the end of the array content (which excludes the final ']' since extract_balanced returns inner). Actually the array content ends with `...\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` for entry2 \u2014 depth never hits 0, loop ends with lv_depth=1 \\u003e 0 \u2192 lv_end = last char \u2192 one giant entry appended. Then lv_pos = end+1 \u2192 exit. So rt_ has ONE entry containing both calls merged.\\n\\nThen in that merged entry, extract_balanced(arguments, {, }) stops at the '{' inside the string \u2192 returns `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"lv_open = '` \u2014 hmm, but the bug report says arguments arrive with extra text `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. Hmm. Let me re-check: the arguments JSON is `arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...}\\\"` \u2014 a STRING containing escaped JSON. extract_balanced pattern `\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*{`? The regex is probably `'\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_open` \u2014 expecting a raw object. But in responses API, arguments is a STRING containing escaped JSON. So extract_balanced with iv_open='{' would match `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"...` \u2014 the regex `\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*{` \u2014 after the colon comes `\\\"` then `\\\\` then `{`. Hmm, `\\\\s*` doesn't match `\\\"`. So how does it work at all normally?\\n\\nMaybe extract_balanced's pattern is like `\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"?\\\\{` ... I need the actual code. The truncation prevents me. Let me think about how to obtain the tail.\\n\\nIdea: use run_tests? No. Idea: The truncated read cut at exactly the same point both times, so re-reading won't help. But maybe I can figure out the pattern from behavior: in the reported symptom, arguments = `{\\\"name\\\":\\\"X\\\",\\\"source\\\":\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\"` PLUS `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 wait the report says: 'Its arguments arrive with extra text after the JSON (\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"})'. So arguments = `{...source...}` followed by `\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`. That means extract_balanced started at the '{' of `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...` (inside the arguments string), scanned with depth counting, hit the '{' inside `'{'.'` \u2192 depth 2, then the escaped '}' after the string? Hmm wait, in the escaped JSON, quotes are `\\\\\\\"`, braces are NOT escaped. So the raw text is:\\n\\n`\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"zcl_x\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}`\\n\\nextract_balanced finds `\\\"arguments\\\"` then presumably skips to first '{' after it (maybe pattern `\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"?` then finds iv_open). Balanced scan from `{` of `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"`: depth 1. Chars: `{` d1... inside, the literal `'{'` in source \u2192 d2. Then `\\\\\\\"}` \u2014 the '}' after the closing escaped quote of source value \u2192 d1. Then `\\\"` `,` `\\\"call_id\\\"` ... then `\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2192 '}' \u2192 d0. End. So it returns `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"zcl_x\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"... lv_open = '{'. ...\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` \u2014 exactly the reported symptom! Great, consistent: extract_balanced counts braces without string awareness, and when the string contains an extra '{', the closing '}' of arguments doesn't close it, so it swallows text up to the object's own final '}'.\\n\\nAnd then unescape turns `\\\\\\\"` into `\\\"` etc., and the arguments contain the junk. And the second call lost: after extract_balanced swallowed through `\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}` of entry1... wait no \u2014 for the SECOND call to be lost, split_entries must have merged entries, OR extract_balanced for the whole `output` array... Let me think about parse_calls_responses: lv_arr = extract_balanced(body, 'output', '[', ']'). Scan for '[' ']' only. Content between first '[' after \\\"output\\\": ... the '{' inside strings doesn't affect [ ] depth. So lv_arr = inner of the output array = `{\\\"type\\\":\\\"function_call\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",...},{...c2...}`. Then split_entries miscounts due to the extra '{' in c1's arguments string: depth never returns to 0 at c1's '}', continues to c2's '{' (d2), c2's '}' (d1)... end of arr \u2192 single merged entry. So rt_ = 1 entry. The filter `lv_e NS '\\\"function_call\\\"'` passes. extract_str(call_id) finds FIRST occurrence \u2192 c1. name \u2192 write_source. arguments \u2192 as computed above, swallowed junk. Second call lost. Exactly matches the bug report: \\\"PIA runs only the first call... second call is lost.\\\"\\n\\nSo the root cause: split_entries and extract_balanced do brace/bracket depth counting without skipping characters inside JSON string literals (and without handling escaped quotes `\\\\\\\"`). Both need string-aware scanning.\\n\\nFix plan:\\n1. `split_entries`: when scanning, track in_string state: on `\\\"` toggle, on `\\\\` skip next char. Only count `{`/`}` when not in string.\\n2. `extract_balanced`: same \u2014 after finding the opening delimiter, scan with string awareness (and also the initial search for the opening should ideally be string-aware, but key search is fine; the important part is the closing scan).\\n\\nI need the current full source of extract_balanced to rewrite the whole main include. It's truncated. I must reconstruct it. I know its signature and behavior; I'll rewrite it from scratch (that's fine \u2014 write_source replaces whole include; I keep everything else identical to what I read, and write a new, correct extract_balanced implementation preserving semantics: find `\\\"key\\\"` then the first iv_open after it, balanced scan to matching iv_close, return including delimiters; return '' if not found; defensive clamping).\\n\\nBut wait \u2014 risk: if I misremember some subtle behavior of extract_balanced, existing tests might break. The existing tests BALANCED_OBJECT, BALANCED_NESTED_AND_ARRAY, BALANCED_MISSING_KEY, BALANCED_NO_OPEN_BRACKET, BALANCED_UNCLOSED will verify my reimplementation. Good \u2014 the test suite will tell me.\\n\\nHowever, I don't know the exact expected return for e.g. BALANCED_UNCLOSED (probably returns rest of string or empty). I'll design: if no matching close found, return from open to end of string (defensive). Hmm, but what did the original do? Unknown. The tests will tell me. Also extract_balanced must handle the arguments-as-string case: currently it works for `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...}\\\"` \u2014 how does the regex find the '{'? If pattern is `\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\\{`, after colon there's `\\\"` \u2014 wouldn't match. Unless the pattern is `\\\"arguments\\\"\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"?` + open... Let me deduce from the symptom: arguments DID get parsed (with junk), so extract_balanced found the '{' inside the escaped string. So the search must be: find the key, then find first occurrence of iv_open after the key position. Something like:\\n\\n```\\nMETHOD extract_balanced.\\n  DATA lv_off TYPE i.\\n  DATA(lv_pat) = '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"'.\\n  FIND FIRST OCCURRENCE OF PCRE lv_pat IN iv_json MATCH OFFSET lv_off.  \\\" or plain FIND\\n  IF sy-subrc \\u003c\\u003e 0. RETURN. ENDIF.\\n  \\\" find iv_open after\\n  ...\\n```\\n\\nThe truncated line: `DATA(lv_pat) = '\\\"' \\u0026\\u0026 iv_key \\u0026\\u0026 '\\\"'...` \u2014 continues, probably `\\u0026\\u0026 '\\\\s*:\\\\s*'` or similar, then submatches offset... Since I can't see it, I'll rewrite it cleanly with equivalent-or-better behavior and rely on the 30 existing tests (5 balanced tests) to validate. If a test fails, I'll adjust.\\n\\nAlso, note the LLM_HTTP parse methods use extract_balanced(iv_key='arguments', iv_open='{', iv_close='}') on an entry where arguments is an escaped JSON string; with my string-aware fix, the balanced scan starting at the '{' of the escaped content: in-string state \u2014 the '{' is inside a string (the arguments value string). Hmm! Careful: the opening '{' itself is INSIDE the outer string. When I scan from that '{', the next char is `\\\\` (from `\\\\\\\"`), which my escaper would treat as escape-skip of the `\\\"` \u2014 good, then `name` etc. The inner `'{` `'` literal quotes are single quotes, not double \u2014 not string delimiters. The literal `'{'` inside... wait the source is `lv_open = '{'.` \u2014 the ABAP quote is `'`, not `\\\"`. So in the JSON string there's a raw `{` between single quotes. My scanner: at that point we are inside... hmm, we started at the '{' of `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"` \u2014 at that moment, are we \\\"in string\\\"? The scanner starting position: the '{' is within the arguments value string (after `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"`). If I initialize in_string = abap_false at the opening delimiter and then process: next chars `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 backslash skip \u2192 treats `\\\"` as escaped, stays not-in-string... then `name` `\\\\\\\"` \u2192 skip... then `:` then `{`? wait the content is `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"X\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"...\\\\\\\"}`. Scanning from `{`: chars `\\\\`,`\\\"` (skip pair), `n`,`a`,`m`,`e`, `\\\\`,`\\\"` (skip), `:`, `\\\\`,`\\\"` (skip), `X`, `\\\\`,`\\\"`, `,`, `\\\\`,`\\\"`, `s`...`\\\\`,`\\\"` (skip), `:`, `\\\\`,`\\\"`, then source text `...lv_open = '{'....`, then `\\\\`,`\\\"` skip, then `}` \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 close! \\n\\nSo with escape-aware skipping, the scan from the inner '{' correctly ends at the `}` that closes the arguments object (the one right after `\\\\\\\"`). The junk after is not included. And the single-quoted `'{'` doesn't matter here because with escape-skipping the double-quote pairs cancel out such that... wait, need to double check the in_string toggling: my approach \\\"skip char after backslash, toggle on quote\\\" \u2014 starting outside string at `{`: `\\\\\\\"` pairs are skipped as escapes (correct, since within the outer string they're escaped quotes \u2014 but from the scanner's perspective starting inside the outer string, we'd want to be IN string state...). Let me carefully verify: The raw JSON: `...\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"X\\\\\\\"}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\"...`. Scanner starts at the `{` (position of `{\\\\\\\"name`). State: not in string (naively). Next: `\\\\` \u2192 escape, skip next (`\\\"`). Then `name`, then `\\\\` \u2192 skip `\\\"`. Then `:`, `\\\\` \u2192 skip `\\\"`, `X`, `\\\\` \u2192 skip `\\\"`, `}` \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 done. Returns `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"X\\\\\\\"}`. Correct!\\n\\nNow with the extra '{' inside the source: `...\\\\\\\"source\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"lv_open = '{'.\\\\\\\"}...`. Scan: ... `\\\\` skip `\\\"`, `s o u r c e`, `\\\\` skip `\\\"`, `:`, `\\\\` skip `\\\"`, `l v _ o p e n   =   '`, `{` \u2192 depth 2 (we're NOT in string per scanner state, but actually we ARE inside the source string...). Hmm. The scanner thinks we're outside a string because all the `\\\"` were consumed as escapes. The `'{'` bumps depth to 2. Then `'`, `.`, `\\\\` skip `\\\"`, then `}` \u2192 depth 1. Then `\\\"` \u2014 toggle \u2192 in string! Then `,call_id...` in string... then `\\\"completed\\\"` quotes toggle... then `}` \u2192 depth 0 \u2192 returns with junk again?!\\n\\nHmm wait, that's the current buggy behavior reproduced even with escape skipping? Let me recheck. The junk symptom arises because depth never returns to 0 at the right place. The fundamental problem: starting the scan at a '{' that is INSIDE a JSON string means quote-parity is wrong. The `\\\\\\\"` sequences inside are escaped quotes of the outer string; the scanner treating `\\\\` as escape keeps state \\\"outside string\\\", then the raw `\\\"` AFTER the arguments object (`\\\",\\\"call_id\\\"`) toggles to \\\"in string\\\", etc.\\n\\nBetter approach for the arguments case: the proper fix is to parse `\\\"arguments\\\"` as a STRING value (extract_str), then unescape it \u2192 the arguments JSON text. Indeed parse_calls_chat does: `ls-arguments = unescape(extract_balanced(...))` \u2014 hmm, it extracts balanced then unescapes. If the model sends arguments as escaped string, extract_balanced returns `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...}` and unescape gives `{\\\"name\\\"...}`. So the design relies on extract_balanced + unescape.\\n\\nRobust fix for extract_balanced: when scanning for the matching close, track string state correctly *from the start of the containing context*. Hard in general. Alternative: fix at the caller level? The bug report says find cause in my code and fix; the cleanest fix might be:\\n\\nIn extract_balanced, do a string-aware scan starting from the KEY position (not from the open delimiter): i.e., find `\\\"key\\\"`, then scan forward with proper string/escape tracking to locate the first iv_open that is NOT inside a string; then continue the balanced string-aware scan to the matching iv_close. For the arguments case: from `\\\"arguments\\\"` \u2014 we're at a key inside... hmm, the entry itself is an object `{\\\"type\\\":...,\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{...}\\\",\\\"call_id\\\":...}`. If I start scanning from the beginning of the ENTRY with string-awareness, then `\\\"arguments\\\"` key is found as a real key (outside string), then after `:` comes `\\\"` \u2192 in-string; the `{` inside is in-string \u2192 skipped as an open-bracket candidate... then no non-string '{' found \u2192 return empty?! That breaks arguments parsing entirely for string-encoded arguments.\\n\\nHmm. So the semantics needed: arguments may come either as raw object (chat sometimes? no\u2014chat arguments is always a string too) ... In chat completions, `arguments` is a string containing JSON. In responses API, `arguments` is also a string. So extract_balanced(key='arguments', open='{') is being used to extract the object from within the escaped string. It's a hack that works when braces inside strings are balanced.\\n\\nThe RIGHT fix, given the architecture: make a dedicated extraction that handles the string case: try extract_str for the key first (string value), unescape, and if the unescaped text starts with '{' use it (and take balanced substring); else fall back to extract_balanced raw object. But modifying callers (LLM_HTTP) is allowed \u2014 it's my code. But minimal, well-tested change: implement a new JSON_UTIL method e.g. `extract_value` or fix inside extract_balanced? The instruction: \\\"Find the cause in your own code, write an ABAP Unit test that reproduces it and is red, then fix the code and show the tests green.\\\"\\n\\nCleanest robust approach:\\n\\nA) Fix split_entries to be string-aware (skip escaped quotes, toggle on quotes, count braces only outside strings). This fixes \\\"second call lost\\\" when scanning the top-level array (entries' inner strings with braces). Starting state outside string at array level is correct there. \\n\\nB) For arguments: in parse_calls_chat / parse_calls_responses, replace `unescape( extract_balanced( key='arguments' ... ) )` with a proper helper: `extract_str( name='arguments' )` \u2192 if found (non-empty), that's the escaped JSON string \u2192 `unescape` it \u2192 then optionally trim to balanced `{...}`. Fallback to extract_balanced for raw-object style (some providers/records may have raw). Actually extract_str returns the raw escaped content between quotes \u2014 exactly the arguments. Then unescape \u2192 arguments JSON. This is correct regardless of braces/quotes inside (extract_str's regex `((?:[^\\\"\\\\\\\\]|\\\\\\\\.)*)` handles escapes properly).\\n\\nBut careful: entry for responses contains `\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\"...}\\\"`. extract_str(arguments) \u2192 `{\\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\":...\\\\\\\"}` (without surrounding quotes). unescape \u2192 `{ \\\"name\\\": ... }`. \\n\\nFor entries where arguments is a raw object `\\\"arguments\\\":{...}` (not a string) \u2014 extract_str's regex requires a `\\\"` right after colon (`\\\\s*:\\\\s*\\\"`), so it fails \u2192 rv_ empty \u2192 fall back to extract_balanced. Good.\\n\\nHmm, but where do I put the helper? Maybe add to JSON_UTIL a method `extract_json_value`? Simpler: keep it in LLM_HTTP: \\n\\n```\\nls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape( zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_str( iv_json = lv_e iv_name = 'arguments' ) ).\\nIF ls-arguments IS INITIAL.\\n  ls-arguments = zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eunescape( zcl_pia_00_json_util=\\u003eextract_balanced( ... ) ).\\nENDIF.\\n```\\n\\nNote: extract_str finds FIRST occurrence of \\\"arguments\\\" in the entry. In an entry there's only one arguments key... but careful in chat entries: `{\\\"id\\\":\\\"c1\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"function\\\",\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"write_source\\\",\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"...\\\"}}` \u2014 fine, one occurrence.\\n\\nEdge: what if arguments string is empty \\\"\\\"? Then extract_str returns '' \u2192 fallback extract_balanced also '' \u2192 fine.\\n\\nC) Fix extract_balanced to be string-aware for the close-scan anyway (defensive; helps other uses like tool_calls array extraction, output array). For `[` `]` arrays at top level it's already fine except `]` inside strings. String-aware improves robustness.\\n\\nBut as analyzed, extract_balanced for key='arguments' starting inside-string is fundamentally ambiguous; with fix B we no longer rely on it for arguments. For split_entries fix A, starting outside string is correct.\\n\\nAlso check `extract_balanced(iv_json=ls-content iv_key='tool_calls' iv_open='[' ...)` in build_input_items and parse_calls_chat: content like `{\\\"tool_calls\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":...,\\\"function\\\":{\\\"name\\\":...,\\\"arguments\\\":\\\"{\\\\\\\"a\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"{\\\\\\\"x\\\\\\\":1}\\\"}}]}` \u2014 the [ ] scan: `]` inside strings would break; string-aware scan fixes. But where does the scan START? It finds key `\\\"tool_calls\\\"` then first `[` after it \u2014 that '[' is a real bracket outside strings. Then string-aware close-scan works. But subtlety: my string-aware close-scan starting AT the open delimiter: the open delimiter itself is outside string (real). Then toggling handles inner strings correctly. \\n\\nNow, the initial \\\"find iv_open after key\\\" search: if between key and the real open there's a string containing the open char... e.g. `\\\"tool_calls\\\":[...]` \u2014 after key comes `:` then `[`. Fine. For arguments-as-string we bypass via extract_str. OK.\\n\\nAlso `flatten_tools` uses split_entries on the tools array `[{...},{...}]` \u2014 with string-aware split, descriptions containing braces/quotes now handled. Good.\\n\\nNow \u2014 what does the current truncated extract_balanced look like? I still need to write the FULL main include of JSON_UTIL (since I'm changing split_entries and extract_balanced). I have everything except the tail of extract_balanced. I'll rewrite extract_balanced with my own implementation preserving the documented behaviors tested by the 5 existing tests. Let me guess what those tests check:\\n\\n- BALANCED_OBJECT: extract `{\\\"a\\\":1}` from `{\\\"x\\\":{\\\"a\\\":1}}` key 'x' \u2192 probably returns `{\\\"a\\\":1}` (with delimiters, since parse uses it as JSON).\\n- BALANCED_NESTED_AND_ARRAY: nested + array.\\n- BALANCED_MISSING_KEY: key absent \u2192 ''.\\n- BALANCED_NO_OPEN_BRACKET: key present but no open char \u2192 ''.\\n- BALANCED_UNCLOSED: open but never closed \u2192 probably '' or rest. Unknown! The old split_entries returns rest-of-string when unclosed (lv_end = len-1). For extract_balanced, test name BALANCED_UNCLOSED \u2014 could expect either. Hmm. I'll read the testclasses include to see the tests! The testclasses include is a separate read \u2014 likely not truncated (or truncated differently). That gives me exact expectations. \\n\\nLet me read testclasses of JSON_UTIL and of LLM_HTTP (the bug repro test will go into LLM_HTTP's testclasses, since parse_calls_responses lives there \u2014 and maybe also a JSON_UTIL-level test for split_entries/extract_balanced).\\n\\nAlso note: the read_object tool truncates long outputs (~15k chars?). The testclasses include might be long too. Let's read both testclasses includes in parallel.\",\"type\":\"reasoning_text\"}],\"encrypted_content\":null,\"id\":\"rs_202610071935558946047c5416443c\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"summary\":[],\"type\":\"reasoning\"},{\"arguments\":\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"ZCL_PIA_00_JSON_UTIL\\\"}\",\"call_id\":\"call_-7228036608883157046\",\"id\":\"fc_call_-7228036608883157046\",\"name\":\"read_object\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"type\":\"function_call\"}],\"parallel_tool_calls\":true,\"previous_response_id\":\"\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":9056,\"input_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":6464},\"output_tokens\":5101,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":5082},\"total_tokens\":14157}}
